  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1216 PM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nThe upper high is advertised to settle more concentrically over NM\ninto Monday with heights reducing a decameter or two, down to\n596-597 at 500 mb. Southeasterly return flow at the surface will\nmeet up with a weak, moist backdoor front on Monday with\nconvergence and upslope flow aligning over northeastern NM where\nstorms would be focused. The east southeast surface winds coupled\nwith west northwesterly winds aloft could yield sufficient\ndirectional shear for a strong to severe storm or two late Monday\nafternoon into the evening. More sparse storms will continue \ndeveloping along and near the Continental Divide Monday, and a \nnear-persistence temperature forecast will keep heat impacts \nfront and center for many NM zones.\n\nA reinforcing backdoor boundary could slide back into southeastern\nCO, southwestern KS, and far northeastern NM with perhaps another\nperturbation aloft along the CO front range on Tuesday. The NAM \nis even more invasive with this backdoor boundary, carrying it \nmuch farther southward into the NM plains. Regardless of exact \nlocation, this boundary will once again help ignite storm \ndevelopment while providing enough directional shear for a few \nmore strong to severe cells Tuesday. By this time, recycling of \navailable moisture would be more difficult for western zones, and \nless coverage is possible there. \n\nTuesday's convection will help govern the placement of the moist,\nunstable plains airmass going into the day Wednesday. For now, it\nis modeled to be draped over northeast to east central NM and\npossibly toward the high terrain of Lincoln county which would\nlead to farther southward expansion of storms Wednesday afternoon. \n\nInto Thursday, the upper high will be settling into southwestern\nNM or eastern AZ. Surface winds look to veer more southeasterly or\nsoutherly with areas along and east of the central mountain chain\nappearing to be the best poised for scattered storms Thursday\nafternoon and evening. Moisture and instability would be then be\ntempered some into Friday with storms tending to stay more focused\nover and near the high terrain.",
